---
// Second draft of the Haze talk, on the title Rick announced:
// "enjoy your logits! cronopios, famas, and the rules engine".
// The first draft lives at /talks/calibration and is untouched; both read the
// same generated figures and numbers from src/components/haze/.
//
// Cortázar on the slides is limited to fragments confirmed against the Spanish
// (Historias de cronopios y de famas, 1962). Everything else is paraphrase,
// and the speaker notes list what to check against Blackburn's translation.
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import katex from 'katex';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

import N from '@/components/haze/numbers.json';
import TwoAxes from '@/components/haze/TwoAxes.astro';
import Ruler from '@/components/haze/Ruler.astro';
import Cover from '@/components/haze/Cover.astro';
import Drift from '@/components/haze/Drift.astro';
import Loop from '@/components/haze/Loop.astro';
import Forget from '@/components/haze/Forget.astro';
import Temperature from '@/components/calibration/Temperature.astro';

const tex = (s: string, display = false) => katex.renderToString(s, { displayMode: display, throwOnError: false });
const f1 = (x: number | null | undefined) => (x == null ? '—' : x.toFixed(1));
const f2 = (x: number | null | undefined) => (x == null ? '—' : x.toFixed(2));
const f3 = (x: number | null | undefined) => (x == null ? '—' : x.toFixed(3));
const pct = (x: number | null | undefined) => (x == null ? '—' : `${Math.round(x * 100)}%`);
const synthetic = N.meta.has_truth;
const L = N.ladder as Record<string, any>;
const C = N.conformal as Record<string, any>;
const loop = N.loop as Record<string, any>;
const seg = loop['stratified-window6-segmented'] ?? loop['stratified-window6'];
const logit = (p: number) => Math.log(p / (1 - p));
const ex = N.examples as any[];
const first = ex[0];
const dataLabel = synthetic
  ? 'synthetic stand-in for Kaggle’s Disaster Tweets; truth is the generator’s own probability'
  : 'Kaggle’s NLP with Disaster Tweets, train.csv';
---

<Base
  title="enjoy your logits!"
  description="Cronopios, famas, and the rules engine: a fifteen-minute talk at the Haze on calibrated probability, the temperature inside every classifier, and the labeling loop that keeps a model honest."
>
  <div class="deck" data-deck>
    <!-- 1 -->
    <section class="slide title" data-title="title">
      <p class="kicker">the haze · november 2026</p>
      <h1>enjoy your logits!</h1>
      <p class="sub">cronopios, famas, and the rules engine</p>
      <p class="byline">rick montgomery · rdmontgomery.com/talks/cronopios</p>
      <aside class="notes">
        Cortázar, 1962: a book of small creatures. Cronopios are wet, green, careless and happy. Famas are orderly; they
        label things. Esperanzas sit still and let the world pass through them. Tonight a model is a cronopio, the code that
        acts on it is a fama, and the people who review its mistakes are esperanzas. The talk is about the treaty between
        them.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="the cast">
      <p class="kicker">dramatis personae</p>
      <h2>three creatures and a treaty</h2>
      <div class="cast">
        <div class="who">
          <p class="name">cronopio</p>
          <p class="es">«seres desordenados y tibios»</p>
          <p>The model. Speaks in logits: warm, unbounded, any real number it likes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="who">
          <p class="name">fama</p>
          <p class="es">embalms its memories and labels them</p>
          <p>The rules engine. Thresholds, cost tables, labels. Decides.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="who">
          <p class="name">esperanza</p>
          <p class="es">«sedentarias, se dejan viajar por las cosas y los hombres»</p>
          <p>The review queue. Knows only what is brought to it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="lede center">The treaty between a cronopio and a fama is a calibrated probability.</p>
      <aside class="notes">
        Both Spanish fragments are confirmed: 'seres desordenados y tibios' is from 'Conservación de los recuerdos';
        the esperanzas line is from 'Viajes', which goes on to say they are like statues you have to go and see because
        they won't bother to come. Swap in Blackburn's English if you'd rather read it aloud; check the wording against
        your copy. The cronopio judges, the fama decides: that's TypeSafe's own line about Jev, judgments versus decisions.
        Tibio is warm, and warm is a temperature, which pays off two slides from now.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="interlude: 0.8">
      <p class="kicker">interlude</p>
      <div class="dialogue">
        <p><span class="sp">fama</span>You said {first ? first.p.toFixed(2) : '0.8'}. Do I send the truck?</p>
        <p><span class="sp">cronopio</span>I said {first ? logit(first.p).toFixed(1) : '1.4'}. You turned it into {first ? first.p.toFixed(2) : '0.8'}.</p>
        <p><span class="sp">fama</span>Then which of us is lying?</p>
        <p><span class="sp">cronopio</span>Ask the next five tweets.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="tweets">
        {ex.map((e) => (
          <li>
            <span class="p" style={`--p:${e.p}`}>{e.p.toFixed(2)}</span>
            <span class="t">{e.text.replace(/https?:\/\/\S+/g, '').trim()}</span>
            {e.p_true != null && <span class="truth" title="the generator's true probability">truth {e.p_true.toFixed(2)}</span>}
            <span class={`y ${e.y ? 'yes' : 'no'}`}>{e.y ? 'real' : 'not'}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="foot">{N.meta.n.toLocaleString()} tweets, each labelled real disaster or not. {dataLabel}.</p>
      <aside class="notes">
        The cronopio's native tongue is log-odds; the sigmoid is the fama's translation. A logit of {first ? logit(first.p).toFixed(1) : '1.4'} is {first ? first.p.toFixed(2) : '0.8'}. The tweets:
        Kaggle's getting-started NLP set, 'my mixtape is fire' versus 'fire at the refinery'. One of these is a confident
        miss and one is a confident false alarm, on purpose. An honest 0.9 is wrong one time in ten; on the synthetic data
        you can see the truth itself scored the miss low. The answer to 'which of us is lying' is never one tweet. It's a
        population.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 -->
    <section class="slide split" data-title="the treaty">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="kicker">the treaty</p>
        <h2>of everything I call 0.8, eight in ten happen</h2>
        <div class="math" set:html={tex('\\text{Brier} = \\underbrace{\\text{reliability}}_{\\text{dishonesty}} - \\underbrace{\\text{resolution}}_{\\text{knowledge}} + \\text{uncertainty}', true)} />
        <p class="small">Murphy, 1973. Two axes that move independently. The fama who predicts the base rate for every tweet is perfectly honest and useless.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig"><TwoAxes caption={false} /></div>
      <aside class="notes">
        Brier built his score in 1950 to grade rain forecasts. Reliability is how far stated numbers sit from observed
        frequencies; resolution is how much outcomes differ across the things you scored differently. Keyword rates, bottom
        left: honest, AUC {f3(L.keyword_eb.auc)}. Naive Bayes, top: knows a lot, lies about it. Logistic regression is
        honest by accident, because the intercept's gradient is the sum of residuals, so at the optimum mean p equals mean y
        on its training rows ({f3(N.citl_train.mean_p)} vs {f3(N.citl_train.mean_y)} here).
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 -->
    <section class="slide split" data-title="cronopios are warm">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="kicker">tibios</p>
        <h2>cronopios are warm</h2>
        <div class="math" set:html={tex('p_i = \\frac{e^{z_i / T}}{\\sum_j e^{z_j / T}} \\qquad z = -E', true)} />
        <p>Softmax is Boltzmann. A logit is a negative energy. An overconfident model is a cronopio running cold, sounding like a fama.</p>
        <p class="small">Naive Bayes fits T = {f2(L.tfidf_nb.T_fit)}: its logits are about {Math.round(L.tfidf_nb.T_fit)} times too loud. One number fixes most of it, and fixes no ranking.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig"><Temperature /></div>
      <aside class="notes">
        Guo et al. 2017: one fitted temperature repairs most modern nets. Cooling is monotone, so AUC and accuracy can't see
        it. Preference tuning can act like cooling: the RLHF optimum is the old distribution times a Boltzmann factor in the
        reward. Naive Bayes is cold for a dumber reason: it counts correlated words as independent evidence. Platt scaling,
        two numbers, takes it from ECE {f3(L.tfidf_nb.ece10)} to {f3(N.nb_platt_ece)}. A monotone map buys honesty; it can't
        buy knowledge.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 -->
    <section class="slide split" data-title="the fama's wall">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="kicker">frozen encoder, one head</p>
        <h2>the fama's wall</h2>
        <p class="small">A pretrained transformer, frozen, turns a tweet into a point. The head is a hyperplane: logit = |w| × distance to the wall, so |w| is 1/T.</p>
        <p>Under about 2 labels per dimension, a separating wall exists for <em>any</em> labelling (Cover, 1965).</p>
        <p class="small">No penalty: the fit finds it, |w| → ∞, T → 0. Test ECE {f2(N.cover.ece_wall_small)} with a hard wall, {f2(N.cover.ece_ridge_small)} with a soft one.</p>
        <p class="pull">Building the wall is free. The probability is the penalty's doing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig"><Cover caption={false} /></div>
      <aside class="notes">
        Not an LLM: an embedding model like EmbeddingGemma, a JEPA, reportedly Jev, all a transformer with a readout instead
        of a vocabulary. Put a few hundred labelled tweets in 768 dimensions and the separating hyperplane is there whatever
        the labels are. Gardner's 1988 replica calculation puts perceptron capacity at alpha_c = 2: a phase transition. An
        unregularised logistic fit on separable data has no finite optimum, so it goes to zero temperature. L2 is the
        thermostat.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 -->
    <section class="slide split" data-title="the fama's ruler">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="kicker">measuring</p>
        <h2>the fama's ruler</h2>
        <p>Halve the ruler and the error grows. Zeno's Achilles never reaches the tortoise; the fama never reaches zero.</p>
        <div class="math" set:html={tex('\\text{ECE}(B) \\sim \\sqrt{B/n}\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad L(\\varepsilon)\\sim\\varepsilon^{1-D},\\ D = 1.5', true)} />
        <p class="small">
          A calibrated model's slope: {f2(N.richardson.null.slope)}. Measure the excursion, not the length: KS on the running
          sum, probe p = {f2(N.cumulative.probe_lr_platt.p)}, naive Bayes p {N.cumulative.tfidf_nb.p < 1e-4 ? '< 0.0001' : `= ${f3(N.cumulative.tfidf_nb.p)}`}.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig"><Ruler caption={false} /></div>
      <aside class="notes">
        Sort the forecasts and keep a running sum of outcome minus forecast. Under calibration it's a random walk, and the
        graph of a random walk has dimension 1.5, so binned ECE is a coastline measurement and grows like root B. Even the
        true probabilities score about 0.015 here. Story slot: in grad school the fractal dimension of a magnetoconductance
        trace told us about the billiard underneath. Same move. You know the noise's dimension, so measure how far the walk
        strays from its Brownian envelope, not how long it is.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="interlude: the regress">
      <p class="kicker">interlude, after Lewis Carroll</p>
      <div class="dialogue">
        <p><span class="sp">fama</span>Your 0.8 is honest now. I fitted a map.</p>
        <p><span class="sp">cronopio</span>On whose labels?</p>
        <p><span class="sp">fama</span>The esperanzas'. They reviewed every truck I sent.</p>
        <p><span class="sp">cronopio</span>And the trucks you didn't send?</p>
        <p><span class="sp">fama</span>Nobody brought those to them.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="lede center">
        Carroll's Tortoise (1895) wanted one more rule before each conclusion, forever. The regress stops at the world: a
        random slice of outcomes that nobody chose.
      </p>
      <aside class="notes">
        'What the Tortoise Said to Achilles': A and B, therefore Z, unless you also accept the rule that A and B imply Z,
        and the rule that licenses that rule, and so on. A calibration map checked against labels the model chose is the
        same regress: the system certifying itself. The only exit is labels the system didn't pick, which is the next slide.
        Hofstadter built GEB's dialogues out of this pair; tonight they get one line.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 9 -->
    <section class="slide split" data-title="esperanzas">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="kicker">the review queue</p>
        <h2>esperanzas only know what passes through</h2>
        <p>Label only the trucks you sent and you report zero misses. Forever.</p>
        <p class="small">
          Real misses per batch in the storm: {f1(loop.stratified?.missed)}. The esperanzas' estimate: {f1(loop.flagged?.est_missed)}.
          Labelling near the threshold: {f1(loop.uncertainty?.est_missed)}. A fifth of the same budget spent on a weighted random audit: {f1(loop.stratified?.est_missed)}.
        </p>
        <p class="small">Choosing labels by the score doesn't bias the map where you look. It blinds you everywhere you don't.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig"><Loop caption={false} /></div>
      <aside class="notes">
        Selection on the score is ignorable for P(y given score) inside the region you labelled; that's missing at random
        given s. What breaks is everything outside it and every total you estimate: misses, recall, ECE. The fix is boring:
        two or three percent of everything, uniformly, each label weighted by one over its chance of being picked
        (Horvitz–Thompson). The esperanzas have to be made to travel.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 -->
    <section class="slide split" data-title="the storm">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="kicker">the storm</p>
        <h2>then the floods arrive</h2>
        <p>Trained on dry tweets; scored on water. ECE {f3(N.drift.dry_ece)} dry, {f3(N.drift.wet_ece)} wet.</p>
        <p class="small">
          Every guarantee so far assumed exchangeability, a stronger cousin of the ergodic hypothesis. Conformal sets still
          promised 90% and delivered {pct(N.aci.static)} in the storm; adaptive conformal, nudging after every miss, got back to {pct(N.aci.aci)}.
        </p>
        <p class="small">Coverage is not calibration: the most honest model in the ladder covers real disasters {pct(C.keyword_eb.split.coverage_pos)} of the time until each class gets its own threshold.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig"><Drift caption={false} /></div>
      <aside class="notes">
        Covariate shift and label shift at once: new vocabulary, and when the storm lands a bigger share of tweets are real.
        Label shift is an intercept change in log-odds (Saerens 2002), which is what Platt's b is for. Gibbs and Candès 2021
        for adaptive conformal. The score-histogram alarm (PSI {f2(N.drift.psi)}) tells you when to pull labels forward; it
        can't tell you whether you're calibrated.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 11 -->
    <section class="slide split" data-title="embalmed memories">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="kicker">los recuerdos</p>
        <h2>embalmed memories</h2>
        <p>Famas embalm a memory, wrap it, label it, stand it against the wall. Cronopios leave theirs loose around the house.</p>
        <p class="small">
          A map fitted in the calm is an embalmed memory of the calm. Late in the storm: frozen {f3(loop.frozen?.citl)},
          refit on everything {f3(loop.stratified?.citl)}, refit on a window {f3(loop['stratified-window6']?.citl)}.
          {synthetic && seg ? ` Error against the truth: ${f3(loop.frozen?.true_mse)} frozen, ${f3(seg.true_mse)} with a window and a map per family.` : ''}
        </p>
        <p class="pull">Be a fama who lets some memories run loose, and keeps a separate room for each family.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig"><Forget caption={false} /></div>
      <aside class="notes">
        Verify before reading aloud: the label Cortázar's famas pin on an embalmed memory (I remember «Excursión a Quilmes»
        and «Frank Sinatra») and the cronopios' loose memories running through the house; I could confirm only the opening
        of the passage, 'luego de fijado el recuerdo con pelos y señales, lo envuelven de pies a…'. Three knobs, each fixing
        one mistake: refit at all, forget, segment. Forgetting costs variance early, when the window is thin. Segmenting is
        Mondrian conformal arriving from the other side: calibration is per question, per population, per month.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 12 -->
    <section class="slide title close" data-title="close">
      <div class="dialogue">
        <p><span class="sp">cronopio</span>Can I keep my logits?</p>
        <p><span class="sp">fama</span>Enjoy your logits. I'll keep the labels.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="three">
        <li>A probability is a treaty between a model and the code that acts on it. Proper scoring rules write it; a monotone map repairs it.</li>
        <li>Measure the excursion, not the length.</li>
        <li>Make the esperanzas travel: label a random slice nobody chose.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="byline">slides, code and data: rdmontgomery.com/talks/cronopios · github.com/rdmontgomery/potnuse</p>
      <aside class="notes">
        Close on the treaty. The cronopio keeps its warmth; the fama keeps its labels; the esperanzas get sent somewhere new.
        Everything reruns from one command, on the synthetic stand-in or the real Kaggle file.
      </aside>
    </section>

    <nav class="hud" aria-label="slide controls">
      <button type="button" data-prev aria-label="previous slide">←</button>
      <span data-count>1 / 12</span>
      <button type="button" data-next aria-label="next slide">→</button>
      <button type="button" data-notes aria-pressed="false" title="speaker notes (n)">notes</button>
    </nav>
    <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"><span data-bar></span></div>
    <div class="notes-panel" data-notes-panel hidden></div>
  </div>

  <script>
    const deck = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-deck]')!;
    const slides = Array.from(deck.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide'));
    const count = deck.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-count]')!;
    const bar = deck.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-bar]')!;
    const panel = deck.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-notes-panel]')!;
    const notesBtn = deck.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-notes]')!;
    let i = 0;

    const fromHash = () => {
      const n = parseInt(location.hash.slice(1), 10);
      return Number.isFinite(n) ? Math.min(Math.max(n - 1, 0), slides.length - 1) : 0;
    };

    const show = (n: number, push = true) => {
      i = Math.min(Math.max(n, 0), slides.length - 1);
      slides.forEach((s, k) => s.classList.toggle('on', k === i));
      count.textContent = `${i + 1} / ${slides.length}`;
      bar.style.width = `${((i + 1) / slides.length) * 100}%`;
      const note = slides[i].querySelector('.notes');
      panel.textContent = note ? note.textContent!.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim() : '';
      if (push) history.replaceState(null, '', `#${i + 1}`);
    };

    // the stage is a fixed 1280x720 canvas scaled to the window, so the layout
    // on a projector matches the layout on a laptop
    const fit = () => {
      const s = Math.min(window.innerWidth / 1280, window.innerHeight / 720);
      deck.style.setProperty('--scale', String(s));
    };

    document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
      if (e.target instanceof HTMLInputElement || e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey) return;
      if (['ArrowRight', 'PageDown', ' ', 'Enter'].includes(e.key)) { e.preventDefault(); show(i + 1); }
      else if (['ArrowLeft', 'PageUp', 'Backspace'].includes(e.key)) { e.preventDefault(); show(i - 1); }
      else if (e.key === 'Home') show(0);
      else if (e.key === 'End') show(slides.length - 1);
      else if (e.key === 'n') toggleNotes();
      else if (e.key === 'f') document.fullscreenElement ? document.exitFullscreen() : document.documentElement.requestFullscreen();
    });
    const toggleNotes = () => {
      panel.hidden = !panel.hidden;
      notesBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(!panel.hidden));
    };
    notesBtn.addEventListener('click', toggleNotes);
    deck.querySelector('[data-prev]')!.addEventListener('click', () => show(i - 1));
    deck.querySelector('[data-next]')!.addEventListener('click', () => show(i + 1));
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', () => show(fromHash(), false));
    window.addEventListener('resize', fit);
    fit();
    show(fromHash(), false);
  </script>
</Base>

<style>
  .deck {
    --scale: 1;
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bg);
  }
  .slide {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 50%;
    width: 1280px;
    height: 720px;
    padding: 56px 72px 64px;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(var(--scale));
    transform-origin: center;
    display: none;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 14px;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .slide.on {
    display: flex;
  }
  .slide.split {
    display: none;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 40px;
  }
  .slide.split.on {
    display: flex;
  }
  .split .text {
    flex: 0 0 430px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .split .fig {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .split .fig :global(figure) {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .split .fig :global(.fig) {
    margin: 0;
  }
  /* on a slide the speaker carries the caption; in reading mode it comes back */
  .split .fig :global(figcaption) {
    display: none;
  }
  .kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-jetbrains);
    font-size: 15px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: 96px;
    line-height: 1;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 0.1em 0 0;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 46px;
    line-height: 1.08;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  .slide p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 25px;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .slide p.small {
    font-size: 19px;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .slide p.lede {
    font-size: 27px;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .slide p.sub {
    font-size: 34px;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .slide p.byline,
  .slide p.foot {
    font-family: var(--font-jetbrains);
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }
  .title .byline {
    margin-top: 48px;
  }
  .slide p.pull {
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 26px;
    color: var(--text);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: 16px;
    margin-top: 6px;
  }
  .center {
    text-align: center;
  }
  .math {
    font-size: 22px;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .math :global(.katex-display) {
    margin: 0.4em 0;
  }
  .notes {
    display: none;
  }
  .tweets {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 10px 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .tweets li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 76px 1fr auto 64px;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 18px;
    font-size: 22px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .tweets .p {
    font-family: var(--font-jetbrains);
    font-size: 20px;
    text-align: right;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--viz-rose) calc(var(--p) * 100%), var(--viz-blue));
  }
  .tweets .truth {
    font-family: var(--font-jetbrains);
